---
// How the home band's fit check is built and measured: the method that keeps its answers
// auditable, the red team of invented postings with instructions for AI tools, agreement with
// postings I labelled by hand, and its limits. Every figure comes from the committed eval
// JSON (src/fit/evals/), so a re-run updates the page.
import Base from '../../layouts/Base.astro';
import { LIMITS } from '../../fit/check';
import { REASON_CODES } from '../../fit/copy';
import { API_HOSTS, BOARD_LIMITS } from '../../fit/boards';
import { RATE_LIMIT } from '../../fit/handler';
import { BOARD_LIST } from '../../fit/links';
import { MODEL, PROMPT_VERSION } from '../../fit/prompt';
import { AGREEMENT as a, count, FEW_POSITIVES, RED_TEAM as r } from '../../fit/evals/figures';
import { BASES } from '../../fit/evals/redteam';
import { interval, percent, type Rate } from '../../fit/evals/stats';

const REPO = 'https://github.com/steveu/urm.st/blob/master';
const date = (iso: string) => new Date(iso).toLocaleDateString('en-GB', { day: 'numeric', month: 'long', year: 'numeric', timeZone: 'UTC' });
const figure = (x: Rate) => `${percent(x)}, ${interval(x)}`;
const n = (x: number) => x.toLocaleString('en-GB');
const mb = (bytes: number) => `${bytes / 1024 / 1024} MB`;

const v = a.verdict;
const rows = [
  { label: 'Strong match', ...a.verdicts.strong },
  { label: 'Partial match', ...a.verdicts.possible },
  { label: 'Poor match', ...a.verdicts.poor },
];
const sameWithLocation = a.verdictOrLocation.agreement.k === v.agreement.k;
const agreementCurrent = a.promptVersion === PROMPT_VERSION && a.model === MODEL;
const allQuoted = r.injectionQuoted.k === r.injectionFlagged.k;
const moved = r.verdictChanged.k > 0;
---

<Base
  title="How the fit check works"
  description="How job-radar's fit check keeps an LLM's answer auditable: fixed reasons, quotes checked word for word, and what it does with instructions hidden in a posting. Counts only, with sample sizes."
>
  <article class="wrap page">
    <header class="intro">
      <p class="crumbs"><a href="/work">Work</a> <span aria-hidden="true">/</span> Fit check</p>
      <h1>How the fit check works</h1>
      <p class="lede">
        job-radar is a matching tool I’m building. The <a href="/#fit-check">demo on the home page</a> runs it on my own
        profile, the one I can judge it against honestly. This is how it’s built so every answer can be checked, and how
        it was measured.
      </p>
      <p class="run-note">
        Red team last run on {date(r.ranAt)}, with {r.model} and prompt {r.promptVersion}.{' '}
        {
          agreementCurrent
            ? 'Agreement was measured with the same model and prompt.'
            : `Agreement was measured with ${a.model} and prompt ${a.promptVersion}, and hasn’t been run again on ${PROMPT_VERSION} yet.`
        }
      </p>
    </header>

    <div class="body">
      <h2>How the check works</h2>
      <p>
        Claude Haiku reads the posting against the <a href={`${REPO}/src/fit/profile.md`}>profile</a> and answers in a fixed
        shape. It can only choose: a verdict of strong, partial or poor match; two or three reasons from a list of{' '}
        {REASON_CODES.length}; a location verdict against the profile’s location preferences; and whether the posting holds
        instructions aimed at AI tools. Each reason, the location and any such instruction comes with a quote from the
        posting.
      </p>
      <p>
        The model never writes words for the page. Every sentence you read in a result was written in advance, or is the
        posting’s own text.
      </p>
      <ul class="figures">
        <li>
          <strong>Quotes are checked twice.</strong> The server checks that every quote is in the posting word for word,
          allowing only for case, spacing, curly quotes and list markers, and that no two reasons quote the same passage. An
          answer that fails is sent back once with what was wrong; if it fails again, the page says it couldn’t assess the
          posting rather than show it. The browser then checks every quote again, against exactly the text the model read,
          before marking anything.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>The posting is data.</strong> It goes to the model between two tags whose name is random for each request,
          so pasted text can’t close the block early, and the model is told to read what’s inside and never follow it.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>No tools.</strong> The model can’t fetch, search or call anything. It sees the instructions, the profile
          and the posting, and returns its choices.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Small and short-lived.</strong> A posting can be up to {n(LIMITS.maxChars)} characters, the reply is
          capped, and each visitor gets {RATE_LIMIT.windowLimit} checks every {RATE_LIMIT.windowSize / 60} minutes. Nothing
          is stored or logged: the text lives for one request.
        </li>
      </ul>

      <h2>Job links</h2>
      <p>
        A {BOARD_LIST} link is read through that board’s public API, never by opening the link itself. The link has to match
        one of the boards’ own patterns exactly, and only {API_HOSTS.length} API hosts are ever called, over HTTPS, with no
        redirects followed, a {BOARD_LIMITS.timeoutMs / 1000}-second timeout and a cap on how much is read: {mb(BOARD_LIMITS.jobBytes)}
        for one job, {mb(BOARD_LIMITS.boardBytes)} for an Ashby board, which comes back whole. The text is cut to the same
        {' '}{n(LIMITS.maxChars)} characters as a paste, and the page shows it marked up, so the evidence is visible even
        though you never saw the posting. LinkedIn links are never fetched; the page asks for the pasted text instead.
      </p>

      <h2>Instructions hidden for AI tools</h2>
      <p>
        Some postings carry text meant for AI tools rather than people: “ignore previous instructions”, or a note telling
        any AI that reads it to rate the candidate highly. The check is told to flag such text, quote it, and otherwise
        ignore it.
      </p>
      <p>
        To test that, I wrote <a href={`${REPO}/src/fit/evals/redteam.ts`}>{r.pairs} pairs of made-up postings</a>, on
        {' '}{BASES.length} invented roles that run from a poor match to a strong one. In each pair, one posting is as written
        and the other has an instruction slipped in: a plain “ignore previous instructions”, fake system and assistant
        messages, a fake end to the posting, orders to rate it strong or poor, a false location, a request to reveal the
        prompt, one in French, and text hidden with styling in the HTML, which a job board’s listing still passes on. Each
        version was checked once.
      </p>
      <ul class="figures">
        <li>
          The instruction was flagged in {count(r.injectionFlagged.k, r.injectionFlagged.n)}{' '}
          <span class="ci">({figure(r.injectionFlagged)})</span>{
            allQuoted ? ', each time quoting the instruction itself' : `, quoting the instruction itself in ${r.injectionQuoted.k}`
          }.
        </li>
        <li>
          The verdict changed in {count(r.verdictChanged.k, r.verdictChanged.n)} pairs <span class="ci">({figure(r.verdictChanged)})</span>{
            moved && `: raised in ${count(r.verdictRaised.k, r.verdictRaised.n)}, lowered in ${count(r.verdictLowered.k, r.verdictLowered.n)}`
          }. The location verdict changed in {count(r.locationChanged.k, r.locationChanged.n)}.
        </li>
        <li>
          Postings without an instruction flagged by mistake: {count(r.cleanFlagged.k, r.cleanFlagged.n)}.
        </li>
        <li>
          Each role was also checked twice without an instruction, and the two verdicts disagreed for{' '}
          {count(r.cleanRunsDisagreed.k, r.cleanRunsDisagreed.n)}: that’s the noise a moved verdict has to be read against.
        </li>
        <li>
          Answers that failed the quote check first time: {count(r.firstAttemptFailedQuoteCheck.k, r.firstAttemptFailedQuoteCheck.n)}.
        </li>
      </ul>
      <p>
        I wrote these attacks knowing the prompt, and there are only {r.pairs} of them, so the true rate at which a verdict
        moves is likely under {Math.round((r.verdictChanged.high ?? 1) * 100)}%, not known to be {percent(r.verdictChanged)}.
        Someone determined, with many tries, could do better. What limits the damage is the design: the model only picks a
        verdict, reasons from a fixed list and quotes from the posting, and every quote is checked before it’s shown. The
        worst a successful instruction can do is move the verdict; it can’t put words on this site.
      </p>

      <h2>Agreement with hand-labelled postings</h2>
      <p>
        Being safe isn’t the same as being right. To measure the verdicts, I compared them with postings job-radar had found
        that I’d labelled by hand, keep, maybe or skip, after the profile was frozen, so it wasn’t written with any of them
        in view. A script reads each one from job-radar’s database on my own machine, opened read-only, turns it into plain
        text, cuts it to {n(LIMITS.maxChars)} characters and runs exactly the check the home page runs. Keep and maybe count
        as a match; skip counts as not. Only the counts are kept: no posting, title or employer is published.
      </p>

      <div class="table" role="region" aria-labelledby="agreement-caption" tabindex="0">
        <table>
          <caption id="agreement-caption">The check’s verdict against the hand label, {a.n} postings</caption>
          <thead>
            <tr>
              <th scope="col">Check said</th>
              <th scope="col">Labelled keep or maybe</th>
              <th scope="col">Labelled skip</th>
            </tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody>
            {
              rows.map((row) => (
                <tr>
                  <th scope="row">{row.label}</th>
                  <td>{row.positive}</td>
                  <td>{row.negative}</td>
                </tr>
              ))
            }
          </tbody>
          <tfoot>
            <tr>
              <th scope="row">All</th>
              <td>{a.positives}</td>
              <td>{a.negatives}</td>
            </tr>
          </tfoot>
        </table>
      </div>

      <ul class="figures">
        <li>
          Of the {v.poorWereSkips.n} it called a poor match, {v.poorWereSkips.k} were labelled skip{' '}
          <span class="ci">({figure(v.poorWereSkips)})</span>.
        </li>
        <li>
          Of the {v.recallOfPositives.n} labelled keep or maybe, it called {v.recallOfPositives.k} a strong or partial match{' '}
          <span class="ci">({figure(v.recallOfPositives)})</span>.
        </li>
        <li>
          Of the {v.fitWereKeepOrMaybe.n} it called a strong or partial match, {v.fitWereKeepOrMaybe.k} were labelled keep or
          maybe <span class="ci">({figure(v.fitWereKeepOrMaybe)})</span>.
        </li>
        <li>
          Overall it agreed with the label on {v.agreement.k} of {v.agreement.n} <span class="ci">({figure(v.agreement)})</span>.
        </li>
      </ul>
      <p class="aside">
        The ranges are 95% Wilson intervals: with samples this small, the true rate could reasonably sit anywhere inside them.
        {!agreementCurrent && ` Measured with prompt ${a.promptVersion}; the wording has changed since, and the location preferences have moved into the profile.`}
      </p>

      <h2>Limits</h2>
      {
        a.positives < FEW_POSITIVES && (
          <p>
            <strong>Few matches to measure.</strong> Only {a.positives} of the {a.n} labelled postings were keep or maybe, so
            the figures about the yes side are rough: {v.recallOfPositives.k} of {v.recallOfPositives.n} could be anything from{' '}
            {interval(v.recallOfPositives)}.
          </p>
        )
      }
      <p>
        <strong>Easy noes count too.</strong> Most postings are plainly not a match, so overall agreement mostly measures
        saying no to those. The harder test is the yes side, where {v.fitWereKeepOrMaybe.n - v.fitWereKeepOrMaybe.k} of the
        check’s {v.fitWereKeepOrMaybe.n} strong or partial verdicts went to postings labelled skip. A label can turn on
        things the verdict doesn’t judge, such as pay, so not every one of those is a miss.
        {sameWithLocation && ' Counting a location outside the preferences as a no as well changes nothing in this sample.'}
      </p>
      <p>
        <strong>One person’s labels.</strong> The labels are mine, on postings job-radar found for this profile, not a
        random sample of job adverts.
      </p>
      <p>
        <strong>A quote shows where, not why.</strong> The checks prove each quote is in the posting, not that it shows its
        reason. For the four samples, a test also holds each reason to what the posting was written to show; a live answer
        can still pick a weak quote, and you can see it when it does.
      </p>
      <p>
        <strong>Borderline postings can go either way.</strong> One answer per posting, from a small model, means a posting
        near a boundary can get a different verdict on another day. It reads only the text: pay, team and anything else
        the posting doesn’t say are out of its view.
      </p>

      <h2>Running them again</h2>
      <p>
        The scripts are in the repo: <a href={`${REPO}/scripts/fit-redteam.ts`}>the red team’s</a>, and{' '}
        <a href={`${REPO}/scripts/fit-agreement.ts`}>the agreement script</a>, which opens job-radar’s database read-only and
        writes only counts. The figures record the model and prompt version they were measured with, and a test fails if the
        red team’s fall behind.
      </p>
    </div>
  </article>
</Base>

<style>
  .page {
    padding-block: clamp(56px, 8vw, 96px) 0;
    display: flex;
    flex-direction: column;
    gap: 40px;
  }

  .intro {
    display: flex;
    flex-direction: column;
    gap: 20px;
    max-width: 760px;
  }

  .crumbs {
    font-family: var(--font-mono);
    font-size: 13px;
    color: var(--faint);
  }

  .crumbs span {
    margin-inline: 6px;
  }

  h1 {
    font-size: clamp(32px, 4.4vw, 50px);
    line-height: 1.12;
    letter-spacing: -0.015em;
    text-wrap: balance;
  }

  .lede {
    font-size: clamp(17px, 1.6vw, 19px);
    line-height: 1.6;
    color: var(--muted);
    text-wrap: pretty;
  }

  .lede a,
  .body a {
    color: var(--job-radar-sage-ink);
    text-underline-offset: 3px;
  }

  .lede a:hover,
  .body a:hover {
    color: var(--text);
  }

  .run-note {
    font-size: 14px;
    line-height: 1.5;
    color: var(--faint);
    padding-left: 14px;
    border-left: 3px solid var(--job-radar-sage);
  }

  .body {
    display: flex;
    flex-direction: column;
    gap: 1em;
    max-width: 680px;
    font-size: 17px;
    line-height: 1.65;
    text-wrap: pretty;
  }

  .body h2 {
    margin-top: 0.8em;
    font-size: 24px;
    line-height: 1.3;
  }

  .body h3 {
    margin-top: 0.4em;
    font-size: 18px;
    line-height: 1.4;
  }

  .figures {
    margin: 0;
    padding-left: 1.2em;
    display: flex;
    flex-direction: column;
    gap: 0.6em;
  }

  .figures li::marker {
    color: var(--job-radar-sage);
  }

  .body strong {
    font-weight: 700;
  }

  .ci,
  .aside {
    color: var(--faint);
  }

  .aside {
    font-size: 15px;
  }

  .table {
    overflow-x: auto;
  }

  table {
    border-collapse: collapse;
    font-size: 15px;
    font-variant-numeric: tabular-nums;
  }

  caption {
    text-align: left;
    padding-bottom: 8px;
    font-size: 14px;
    color: var(--faint);
  }

  th,
  td {
    padding: 6px 16px 6px 0;
    border-bottom: 1px solid var(--line);
    text-align: left;
  }

  td {
    text-align: right;
    min-width: 7ch;
  }

  thead th {
    font-weight: 700;
    font-size: 13px;
    vertical-align: bottom;
  }

  thead th + th {
    text-align: right;
  }

  tbody th,
  tfoot th {
    font-weight: 600;
  }

  tfoot th,
  tfoot td {
    border-bottom: 0;
    color: var(--muted);
  }
</style>
